Package: xemu-xbox
Architecture: amd64
# xemu statically links the SDL3 it vendors as a meson subproject, and that SDL3
# dlopens its video/audio backends rather than linking them. dh_shlibdeps cannot
# see a dlopen, so none of the following are picked up automatically and they
# must be listed by hand. libvulkan1 is the important one: Vulkan is xemu's
# renderer, so without it the package installs and then fails at startup.
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends}, ${misc:Depends},
 libvulkan1,
 libx11-6,
 libx11-xcb1,
 libxcursor1,
 libxext6,
 libxfixes3,
 libxi6,
 libxrandr2,
 libxss1,
 libegl1,
 libgl1,
 libgbm1,
 libdrm2
# The Vulkan ICD is what actually provides a device to the libvulkan1 loader; on
# anything but a Mesa-driven GPU the user supplies their own vendor driver, so
# this is a strong recommendation rather than a hard dependency. Audio and
# Wayland decorations are likewise dlopened and individually optional.
Recommends: mesa-vulkan-drivers,
 libasound2t64,
 libpulse0,
 libpipewire-0.3-0t64,
 libdecor-0-0,
 libdbus-1-3
Description: original Xbox game console emulator
 Note that an unrelated 8-bit computer emulator suite is also called
 "xemu" (https://github.com/lgblgblgb/xemu) and has shipped its own .deb
 under that name since 2016; it is packaged here as xemu. This package is
 the original Xbox emulator from https://xemu.app, qualified as xemu-xbox
 to disambiguate. The binary it installs is still /usr/bin/xemu.
 xemu is an open-source emulator of the original Microsoft Xbox (2001), built
 as a fork of QEMU that models the console's NV2A GPU, MCPX boot ROM, APU audio
 hardware, and IDE/USB peripherals rather than high-level-emulating its titles.
 .
 Because it descends from QEMU's i386 system emulation, xemu runs the real Xbox
 kernel and title code, which makes it a preservation-grade reference as well
 as a practical way to run original-Xbox software. Rendering goes through
 Vulkan; audio through SDL2 or PipeWire.
 .
 xemu requires an Xbox MCPX boot ROM and BIOS image plus a hard-disk image to
 run. These are copyrighted Microsoft material and are NOT shipped with this
 package — you must supply them yourself, from hardware you own. Configure
 their paths on first launch via the built-in settings dialog.
 .
 This package ships the emulator as /usr/bin/xemu along with a desktop entry
 and hicolor icons, so it appears in the applications menu.
